course: history
lesson: 7
title: Ancient India: the Indus cities and the first kingdoms
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: About 4,500 years ago, while the pyramids were being built, great cities stood by the river Indus, in today's Pakistan and northwest India: Harappa and Mohenjo-daro.
explain: They were planned like a modern town: straight streets in a grid, bricks all the same size, and houses with bathrooms and toilets joined to covered drains. Many towns in Europe had nothing like it for thousands of years.
explain: The Indus people wrote short signs on little stone seals with animals on them, but nobody can read their writing yet, like a locked diary with no key.
explain: The cities slowly emptied, probably as rivers changed course and the climate dried. Later, kingdoms grew along the Ganges, the Buddha taught about suffering and kindness, and the Maurya family built an empire across most of India.
fact: About 4,500 years ago great cities such as Harappa and Mohenjo-daro stood by the river Indus, in today's Pakistan and northwest India.
fact: The Indus cities had straight streets in a grid and houses of baked bricks all of the same size.
fact: Many Indus houses had bathrooms and toilets connected to covered drains along the streets, which carried dirty water away from people.
fact: Mohenjo-daro had a large waterproof pool called the Great Bath, probably used for washing in religious ceremonies.
fact: The Indus people used seals with short inscriptions and animal pictures, but nobody has yet worked out their writing.
fact: Around 3,900 years ago the Indus cities slowly emptied, perhaps because rivers changed course and the climate became drier.
fact: An old idea that invaders destroyed the Indus cities suddenly is now not believed; the decline was slow.
fact: The Vedas are very old sacred hymns, learned by heart and passed on by speaking for many centuries before being written down.
fact: Later many kingdoms grew up along the river Ganges, with cities, kings and markets.
fact: Siddhartha Gautama, a prince, left his palace about 2,500 years ago to understand why people suffer, and became known as the Buddha, the awakened one.
fact: Chandragupta Maurya built an empire covering most of India about 2,300 years ago.
fact: The Indus cities were at their height around the same time the Great Pyramid was built in Egypt.
exercise: Which river did the great early cities of India stand by? | The Indus | The Nile in Egypt | The Tigris river | The Thames
exercise: In which modern countries were the Indus cities? | Pakistan and northwest India | Egypt and Sudan, along the Nile | China and Japan | Iraq and Iran
exercise: Name one of the great Indus cities. | Mohenjo-daro | Uruk in Sumer | Babylon | Jericho
exercise: About how long ago were the Indus cities great? | About 4,500 years ago | About 450 years ago | About 45 years ago | About 45,000 years ago
exercise: How were the streets of the Indus cities laid out? | Straight, in a grid | Round, in circles | Wherever anyone liked | Only along the river bank
exercise: What was special about Indus bricks? | They were baked and all the same size | They were made of solid gold from the hills | They were all different shapes | They were made of ice
exercise: Where did Indus bathrooms and toilets drain to? | Covered drains along the streets | Straight into the kitchen next door | Nowhere, they had none | Into holes on the flat roof
exercise: What was the Great Bath? | A waterproof pool, probably for religious washing | A huge bathtub for the king alone | A swimming race for the Olympics | A big water tank for the ships on the river to fill up from
exercise: Can we read the Indus writing today? | No, nobody has worked it out yet | Yes, it is the same as English | Yes, the Rosetta Stone solved it | No, because they never wrote anything
exercise: What was found on Indus seals? | Short inscriptions and animal pictures | Maps of the whole world | Portraits of the Roman emperors, one on each | Long stories about kings
exercise: Why did the Indus cities empty? | Probably rivers changing course and a drier climate | A sudden invasion destroyed them in one day | A giant flood of lava | Everyone moved to Egypt by boat
exercise: How were the Vedas passed on for centuries? | By being learned by heart and spoken | By being printed in books | By being carved on the walls of the pyramids | By being sent in letters
exercise: Along which river did later Indian kingdoms grow? | The Ganges | The Amazon in Brazil | The Danube in Europe | The Euphrates
exercise: Who was the Buddha before he left his palace? | A prince named Siddhartha Gautama | A poor fisherman named Ashoka | A Roman soldier | A Chinese emperor
exercise: What does the name Buddha mean? | The awakened one | The strong old king | The rich young prince | The great builder of temples
exercise: Who built an empire covering most of India about 2,300 years ago? | Chandragupta Maurya | Cyrus the Great of Persia | Qin Shi Huang of China | Alexander the Great of Macedon
test: Why would covered drains make a city healthier? | Dirty water is kept away from people | Drains make the streets warmer in winter | Drains keep out robbers | Covered drains grow food
test: An old book says invaders wiped out the Indus cities in one day. What do historians think now? | The cities faded slowly, probably from river and climate changes | The book is right in every detail | Volcanoes destroyed them | Aliens took the people away
test: You dig up a seal with a bull and signs nobody can read. Where was it probably from? | The Indus Valley cities | Ancient Rome | Viking Norway | The Aztec Empire in Mexico
test: If every brick is the same size, what does that suggest? | Someone planned and organised the building | The bricks were all made by one person alone | Nobody cared how houses looked | Bricks grew on trees
test: so was india like super behind egypt back then or what? | No, the Indus cities were great at the same time as the pyramids | Yes, India had no cities at all until the Romans came to build them | Yes, India was empty then | No, India had cars already
test: Why did the prince Siddhartha leave his comfortable palace? | To understand why people suffer | To become a soldier for Rome | To find gold in the mountains | Because the palace burned down
test: The Vedas were not written down for centuries. How did they survive? | People learned them by heart and recited them | They were kept in a cave as painted pictures on the walls | They were sung only by birds | They did not survive at all
test: What would happen if someone found a long Indus text written next to a known language? | It might help us read the Indus writing | It would prove the Indus people were Romans | Nothing, no writing can ever be read | All the cities would come back
test: Most towns in Europe 4,500 years ago had no drains. Which is true? | The Indus cities were far ahead in planning | Europe had much better drains than India did | Nobody anywhere had houses then | The Indus cities had no houses
test: A gamer says India in history was always one country under one king. Is that right? | No, there were many kingdoms; empires like the Maurya came and went | Yes, always one king since the start | Yes, the Indus king ruled until today | No, India had no kings at all
exam: Identify the river along which the cities of Harappa and Mohenjo-daro were situated. | The Indus | The Ganges | The Yangtze | The Euphrates
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the urban arrangements of the Indus civilisation? | Streets laid out upon a grid, with standard bricks and covered drains | Circular streets without drainage of any kind | Wooden huts disposed at random | Towers of glass and iron
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the script of the Indus civilisation. | It has not hitherto been deciphered | It was deciphered by means of the Rosetta Stone | It is identical with Greek | It was never employed upon seals
exam: Ascertain the explanation of the decline of the Indus cities which is at present most widely accepted. | A gradual abandonment attributable to changing rivers and a drier climate | A sudden destruction by invaders in a single day | A volcanic eruption | A decree of the Persian king
